\section{Ordinal responses}{

\code{cumulative()}, \code{sratio()}, \code{cratio()} and \code{acat()} have no mean on
the response scale, so \code{type = "response"} (and its alias
\code{type = "conditional"}) returns an \verb{n x K} matrix of category
probabilities instead of a vector - the brms \code{fitted()} convention -
with the response's own factor levels as column names. The rows sum
to one. \code{cs()} category-specific terms are honored: they enter each
threshold separately and are re-evaluated on \code{newdata}.

\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} returns the same matrix, so the usual
\code{predict(type = "response") == fitted()} identity holds here too.

\code{type = "link"} (the default) and \code{dpar = "mu"} still give the latent
linear predictor, which is where the fixed-effect coefficients live
and where \code{se.fit} is available. \code{se.fit} on the response scale is
refused: the prediction is a K-vector per row, not one number.
\code{emmeans} and \code{insight::get_predicted()} stay on that latent scale,
which is the \code{mode = "latent"} convention for \code{clm}-like models.

\code{type = "conditional"} is glmmTMB's name for the conditional MEAN, so
it gives the category probabilities here too rather than the linear
predictor: an ordinal response has no mean, and answering a question
about a mean with a latent predictor is the confusion this section
exists to remove. Ask for the predictor by name (\code{type = "link"}, or
\code{dpar = "mu"}) when that is what you want.
}

\section{What \code{re.form = NA} drops}{

\code{re.form = NA} (equivalently \code{~0}) asks for the POPULATION-level
prediction. Every \code{(x | g)} block is dropped, and so is any smooth
whose basis gives each level of a grouping factor its own curve.
Everything else stays.

Dropped:
\itemize{
\item \code{(1 | g)}, \code{(x | g)}, and the structured spellings of them
(\code{gr()}, \code{cs()}, \code{ar()}, \code{mm()}, \code{car()}, \code{spde()}, ...).
\item \code{s(t, g, bs = "fs")}, the factor-smooth interaction: one curve per
level of \code{g}, so the curves ARE the group deviations.
\item \code{s(g, bs = "re")} and \code{s(x, g, bs = "re")}, which are a random
intercept and a random slope written as a smooth.
\item \code{t2(t, g, bs = c("cr", "re"))} and any other tensor product with an
\code{re} margin, which is the same random smooth in a different
spelling.
}

Kept:
\itemize{
\item \code{s(t)}, \code{s(t, by = x)}, \code{te()}, \code{t2()}, and every other population
smooth. A smooth's wiggly part is stored as a random-effect block
because that is how a penalty is written as a mixed model, but the
term is a population effect and the population prediction is the
fitted curve, not the null-space line through it.
\item \code{gp()} and \code{hsgp()} terms.
}

The test is what the basis MEANS, not the \code{bs} string: \code{bs = "sz"}
names a factor the way \code{bs = "fs"} does, but writes the level curves
as contrasts against a reference level, which is mgcv's spelling for
a factor whose levels are fixed effects, so it would count as
population-level. (\code{sz} has no random-effect representation, so it is
not fittable here at all; the classification is stated for
completeness.)

The result is \code{mgcv::predict.gam(exclude = )} on the factor-smooth
term, and \code{tests/testthat/test-smooth-population.R} asserts the two
agree to 1e-6 on a shared fit.

This deliberately follows mgcv rather than brms: brms stores every
smooth's wiggly part as population parameters, so its
\code{re_formula = NA} KEEPS factor-smooth curves. A ported brms call
with a \code{bs = "fs"} term therefore returns different numbers here,
on purpose: the retained per-level curve is not a population
quantity, and mgcv, the authority frmtmb's smooth estimation
already follows, drops it too.

A dropped factor-smooth term needs nothing from \code{newdata}, so the
grouping column may be left out entirely when \code{re.form = NA}. It is
required for a conditional prediction, and its absence is reported by
name rather than by an mgcv internal message.

\code{conditional_effects()} draws its curves at the population level, so
it follows this rule too: on a model with a factor-smooth term the
displayed curve is the population smooth, and the grouping factor is
not offered as an effect to plot.
}

\section{Standard errors of the expected response}{

For a family whose mean is the \code{mu} dpar, \code{se.fit} on
\code{type = "response"} is the usual one-predictor delta method:
\verb{|dmu/deta| * se(eta)}.

When the mean is a function of several dpars (zero-inflated and
hurdle families, \code{lognormal}, a \code{trials()} binomial, or any
\code{trunc()}ed response), the delta method runs jointly over every
dpar's linear predictor: \verb{se^2 = g' V g}, where row \code{i} of \code{g}
stacks \code{dm_i/deta_k} times the design row of predictor \code{k}, and \code{V}
is the joint covariance of all the coefficients (\code{vcov()}'s
\code{jointPrecision} block, so the cross-predictor covariances and the
shared random-effect block are included). The gradients
\code{dm/deta_k} are central differences of the family mean, taken one
predictor at a time with a relative step.

Random effects enter conditional on their modes, the same convention
\code{se.fit} uses for the linear predictor. Unseen grouping levels
(\code{allow_new_levels = TRUE}) add their block's marginal variance,
propagated through the same gradients.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)
dd <- data.frame(x = rnorm(100), g = factor(rep(1:10, 10)))
dd$y <- rpois(100, exp(0.3 + 0.4 * dd$x + rnorm(10, 0, 0.6)[dd$g]))
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + (1 | g)) + poisson(), data = dd)

# the link scale by default; "response" is what fitted() returns
head(predict(fit))
max(abs(predict(fit, type = "response") - fitted(fit)))

# re.form = NA drops the random effects: the population prediction
nd <- data.frame(x = c(-1, 0, 1), g = factor(1, levels = levels(dd$g)))
predict(fit, newdata = nd, re.form = NA, type = "response")

# delta-method standard errors, on whichever scale was asked for
p <- predict(fit, newdata = nd, se.fit = TRUE)
cbind(fit = p$fit, se = p$se.fit)

# a level the fit never saw errors unless it is allowed explicitly,
# in which case it is predicted at the population level
nd_new <- data.frame(x = 0, g = factor("new"))
try(predict(fit, newdata = nd_new))
predict(fit, newdata = nd_new, allow_new_levels = TRUE)

# a distributional parameter instead of the mean
fit2 <- frm(bf(y ~ x, sigma ~ x) + gaussian(), data = dd)
head(predict(fit2, dpar = "sigma", type = "response"))
}
